## Tutorials

This is how it works:

When JavaScript reaches abreakkeyword, itbreaks out of the switchblock.

This willstop the executioninside the switch block.

No more statements in the switch block will be executed.

It is not necessary to break the last case. The switch ends (breaks) there anyway.

Thedefaultkeyword specifies a block of code to run if there is no 
case match.

Thedefaultkeyword is optional.

Thedefaultcan act as a fallback:

Thedefaultcase does not have to be the last case in a switch 
block:

Sometimes you will want different 
switch cases to use the same 
code.

In this example case 4 and 5 share the same code block, and 0 and 6 share 
another code block:

If multiple cases matches a case value, thefirstcase is selected.

If no matching cases are found, the program continues to thedefaultlabel.

If no default label is found, the program continues to the statement(s)after the switch.

Switch usesstrictcomparison (===).

The values must be of the same type to match.

A strict comparison can only be true if both operands are of the same type.

